==Old Norse==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|daujan|lang=non}}, whence also Old High German {{term|touwen}}.

===Verb===
{{infl|non|verb}}

# to [[die]]

#* '''Deyr''' fé, / '''deyja''' frændr, / '''deyr''' sjalfr it sama, / en orðstírr / '''deyr''' aldregi, / hveim er sér góðan getr. &mdash; verse 76 of the ''[[w:Hávamál|Hávamál]]''
#*: Cattle '''die''', / kinsmen '''die''' / the self must also '''die'''; / I know one thing which never '''dies''': / the reputation of each dead man. &mdash; 1996 translation by Carolyne Larrington


